      {
        "id": "s2_forces",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "The two forces",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Two forces",
        "lines": [
          "Orbital velocity is the velocity with which a",
          "body must revolve to stay in a circular orbit.",
          "The gravitational pull of the planet supplies",
          "exactly the centripetal force the satellite",
          "needs, so the two are equal:",
          {
            "text": "m v₀² / (R + h) = G M m / (R + h)²",
            "style": "eq"
          }
        ],
        "why": "A satellite is not held up by anything — it is falling, and falling at just the rate that keeps the ground curving away beneath it.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Adding the two forces instead of setting them equal."
        ],
        "recall": {
          "credit": "say_it",
          "must_convey": "That the gravitational pull equals the centripetal force, m v naught squared by R plus h equals G M m by R plus h squared.",
          "accept": [
            "gravitational pull provides the centripetal force",
            "m v squared by R plus h equals G M m by R plus h squared"
          ],
          "reject": [
            "draw the planet with the satellite"
          ],
          "heard_as": [
            "centripetal",
            "equate"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_expression",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "The expression",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Expression",
        "lines": [
          "Cancelling m and one factor of (R + h),",
          {
            "text": "v₀ = √(G M / (R + h))",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          "For an orbit close to the surface h is small",
          "and G M = g R², so",
          {
            "text": "v₀ = √(gR) ≈ 7.9 km s⁻¹",
            "style": "boxed"
          }
        ],
        "why": "Compare with escape velocity: v_e = √2 × v₀, so escaping needs only about 1.4 times the orbital speed.",
